<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Material Symbols Outlined "drag_handle", Apache-2.0.
  Source: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/google/material-design-icons/master/symbols/web/drag_handle/materialsymbolsoutlined/drag_handle_24px.svg

  Path data is byte-for-byte the upstream `d` attribute. The source viewBox is
  already "0 -960 960 960" — the same 960 grid this drawable uses — so there is
  no rescaling here (nothing x40 or otherwise): the glyph is moved into the
  viewport with a single translateY, exactly as `ic_download.xml` does.

  THE GEOMETRY, for the "is it too thin at 24dp?" question. The glyph is two
  bars, each 640 wide x 80 tall, spanning x 160..800 and y -600..-360:
    upper bar  M160-520 ... y -600..-520
    lower bar  M160-360 ... y -440..-360
  An 80/960 bar is 2.0dp at 24dp — the ordinary Material stroke weight at this
  size (the tray bar in `ic_download.xml` is also 80 units, i.e. 2dp), and the
  gap between the two bars is likewise 80 units. So the lines are not thinned
  by the grid conversion; they are standard weight. What makes the glyph read
  as delicate is that it is only two bars — the ink covers a 16dp x 6dp patch
  in the middle of the box — not the bar thickness.

  Filled white, not tinted, as the convention spelled out in
  `ic_orientation_landscape.xml` requires for this icon set. Note that the one
  call site so far, the reorder handle of a playlist entry row in
  `ui/common/EntryList.kt`, does pass `tint = onSurfaceVariant`, which overrides
  this fill; the hard-coded white keeps the file consistent with its neighbours
  and is what would show anywhere that does not tint.

  Shown at the trailing end of a row inside the playlist / queue list, as the
  grip for reordering: the drag gesture is attached to this handle alone, so
  touching the rest of the row still does what it always did. Two stacked bars
  are the conventional "drag to move" mark.
-->
<vector xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    android:width="24dp"
    android:height="24dp"
    android:viewportWidth="960"
    android:viewportHeight="960">
    <!-- The source viewBox starts at y = -960; shift it into the viewport. -->
    <group android:translateY="960">
        <path
            android:fillColor="#FFFFFFFF"
            android:pathData="M160-360v-80h640v80H160Zm0-160v-80h640v80H160Z" />
    </group>
</vector>
